tasks.output:
JSON payloads

1) List tasks
{
  "method": "GET",
  "url": "https://api.example.com/tasks"
}

2) Create a task
{
  "method": "POST",
  "url": "https://api.example.com/tasks",
  "headers": {
    "Content-Type": "application/json"
  },
  "body": {
    "title": "Buy milk",
    "completed": false
  }
}

3) Update a task
{
  "method": "PUT",
  "url": "https://api.example.com/tasks/{taskId}",
  "path_params": {
    "taskId": "123"
  },
  "headers": {
    "Content-Type": "application/json"
  },
  "body": {
    "title": "Buy milk and eggs",
    "completed": true
  }
}

4) Delete a task
{
  "method": "DELETE",
  "url": "https://api.example.com/tasks/{taskId}",
  "path_params": {
    "taskId": "123"
  }
}

Explanation
- These define a simple CRUD REST API for a to-do list.
- No authentication fields are included; omit api_key_* for public/local development.
- Paths use a {taskId} placeholder for operations on a single task.

Setup instructions
- No secrets needed (no authentication used). If you later add auth, pick one pattern (e.g., Bearer) and provide api_key_* fields accordingly.

Expected responses
- List (GET /tasks): 200 OK, body: [{"id":"123","title":"Buy milk","completed":false}, ...]
- Create (POST /tasks): 201 Created, body: {"id":"124","title":"Buy milk","completed":false}
- Update (PUT /tasks/{taskId}): 200 OK, body: {"id":"123","title":"Buy milk and eggs","completed":true}
- Delete (DELETE /tasks/{taskId}): 204 No Content (or 200 with {"deleted":true})
